{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"pair-tuple","title":"pair & tuple","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":55,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::pair&lt;A, B&gt;</code> holds two values; <code>std::tuple&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> holds any number. Both compare lexicographically, which makes them handy as keys and sort criteria.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i><code>std::pair</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::pair&lt;std::string, int&gt; p{\"ada\", 36};\n    auto q = std::make_pair(std::string(\"bob\"), 25);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; p.first &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p.second &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (p &lt; q) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;std::pair&lt;int, char&gt;&gt; v{{2, 'b'}, {1, 'z'}, {2, 'a'}};\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                  // by first, then by second\n    for (const auto&amp; [n, c] : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; c &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ada 36 1\n1z 2a 2b</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i><code>std::tuple</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;tuple&gt;\n\nstd::tuple&lt;int, int, int&gt; minMaxSum(int a, int b, int c) {\n    return {std::min({a, b, c}), std::max({a, b, c}), a + b + c};\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::tuple&lt;std::string, int, double&gt; t{\"x\", 1, 2.5};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::get&lt;0&gt;(t) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::get&lt;int&gt;(t) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::tuple_size_v&lt;decltype(t)&gt; &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto [lo, hi, sum] = minMaxSum(4, 9, 2);        // structured bindings\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; lo &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; hi &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int a, b;\n    std::tie(a, b, std::ignore) = minMaxSum(1, 2, 3);   // unpack into existing variables\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto joined = std::tuple_cat(std::make_tuple(1, 'c'), std::make_tuple(2.5));\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::get&lt;2&gt;(joined) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>x 1 3\n2 9 15\n13\n2.5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Multi-key comparison with <code>std::tie</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;tuple&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Student { std::string name; int grade; int age; };\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;Student&gt; s{{\"cy\", 90, 20}, {\"al\", 85, 22}, {\"bo\", 90, 19}};\n    std::sort(s.begin(), s.end(), [](const Student&amp; a, const Student&amp; b) {\n        return std::tie(b.grade, a.age) &lt; std::tie(a.grade, b.age);   // grade desc, then age asc\n    });\n    for (const auto&amp; st : s) std::cout &lt;&lt; st.name &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>bo cy al</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>For anything with meaning, prefer a small named <code>struct</code> over <code>pair</code>/<code>tuple</code>: <code>result.quotient</code> reads better than <code>std::get&lt;0&gt;(result)</code>. Tuples shine in generic code and quick multi-value returns.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do pairs and tuples compare?</summary><div><p>Lexicographically: compare the first elements; only if they're equal compare the second, and so on. This is why sorting a <code>vector&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt;</code> sorts by <code>first</code> then <code>second</code>, and why <code>std::tie(a.x, a.y) &lt; std::tie(b.x, b.y)</code> is the idiomatic multi-field comparison.</p></div></details></section>"}